 "bloques": [
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "Much y many",
    "@en": "Much and many"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "Para preguntar por el número de unidades, usa **how many** con sustantivos contables en plural: **How many eggs?** Para preguntar por una cantidad no contable, usa **how much**: **How much milk?**, **How much rice?**",
     "@en": "To ask for the number of units, use **how many** with plural accountants: **How many eggs?** to ask for a non-accounting amount, use **how much**: **How much milk?**, **How much rice?**"
    },
    {
     "@es": "**How much is it?** es una pregunta fija por el precio; también se dice **How much money?** porque **money** es incontable. **How many pounds?** pregunta por el número de libras.",
     "@en": "**How much is it?** is a fixed question for the price; it is also said **How much money?** because **money** is countless. **How many pounds?** asks about the number of pounds."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "Mucho y poco",
    "@en": "A lot and a little"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "**A lot of** (mucho, muchos) vale para sustantivos contables e incontables: **a lot of eggs**, **a lot of milk**. En afirmativas normalmente preferimos **a lot of** a **much/many**.",
     "@en": "**A lot of** (much, many) is valid for accounting and countless nouns: **a lot of eggs**, **a lot of milk**. In Affirms we usually prefer **a lot of** to **much/many**."
    },
    {
     "@es": "En negativas usamos **not many** (no muchos, con contables) y **not much** (no mucho, con incontables).",
     "@en": "In negatives we use **not many** (not many, with accountants) and **not much** (not much, with countless)."
    },
    {
     "@es": "**Need** es «necesitar» y **buy** es «comprar». **Let's** + verbo sirve para proponer: **Let's go!** (¡vamos!).",
     "@en": "**Need** is «need» and **buy** is «buy». **Let's** + verb serves to propose: **Let's go!** (come on!)."
    }
   ]
  },
